---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ContentSection from '../components/ContentSection.astro';
import LinkCard from '../components/LinkCard.astro';

const relatedPages = [
  { title: 'Public vs Sharp', href: '/public-vs-sharp-money', description: 'Who moves markets' },
  { title: 'How Odds Work', href: '/how-betting-odds-work', description: 'Understanding value' },
  { title: 'Common Mistakes', href: '/common-betting-mistakes', description: 'Avoiding errors' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="When Popular Bets Are Overpriced"
  description="Understanding why heavily bet sides sometimes offer poor value and how public bias can create pricing inefficiencies."
>
  <Hero
    title="When Popular Bets Are Overpriced"
    subtitle="Public Bias and Pricing Inefficiencies"
    size="medium"
  />

  <div class="container content-container">
    <article class="page-content">
      <ContentSection>
        <div class="quick-take">
          <h2>Quick Take</h2>
          <p>
            When betting money flows heavily toward one side, sportsbooks may shade lines to reduce risk or capture extra margin. This can make popular sides overpriced relative to fair value. However, the relationship between public betting and value is nuanced—simply fading the public isn't a winning strategy because markets are efficient and public bias is often already priced in.
          </p>
        </div>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Why Can Popular Sides Be Overpriced?" id="why">
        <h3>Public bias toward favorites</h3>
        <p>
          Casual bettors prefer favorites and popular teams. Sportsbooks know this and may shade lines accordingly.
        </p>

        <h3>Narrative-driven betting</h3>
        <p>
          Media storylines push bettors toward certain sides, creating demand that can inflate prices beyond fair value.
        </p>

        <h3>Sportsbook risk management</h3>
        <p>
          When too much money sits on one side, books may move lines to attract opposite action or simply accept the lopsided exposure with padding.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What This Means for Bettors" id="what-means">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Don't assume popular = wrong:</strong> Public favorites win games too. The question is whether the price is right.</li>
          <li><strong>Value requires your own analysis:</strong> Fading the public only works if you have a reason to disagree.</li>
          <li><strong>Markets are efficient:</strong> Simple contrarian strategies don't work because the market already accounts for public bias.</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Key Takeaways" id="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Heavy public action can inflate prices on popular sides</li>
          <li>Simply fading the public isn't a profitable strategy</li>
          <li>Value comes from analysis, not contrarianism for its own sake</li>
          <li>Markets often already account for public bias</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Related Resources" id="related">
        <div class="related-grid">
          {relatedPages.map((page) => (
            <LinkCard title={page.title} href={page.href} description={page.description} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </ContentSection>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-content { padding: var(--spacing-3xl) 0; }
  .quick-take { background-color: var(--color-surface); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent); padding: var(--spacing-lg); border-radius: 0 var(--border-radius) var(--border-radius) 0; margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl); }
  .quick-take h2 { font-size: 1rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--color-accent); margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm); }
  .quick-take p { margin: 0; color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .related-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: var(--spacing-md); margin-top: var(--spacing-lg); }
  @media (max-width: 768px) { .related-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
